# 🗄️ Message Persistence

Real-time communication alone is not enough.

If messages existed only inside Socket.IO memory, they would disappear when the server restarted.

Therefore, messages are also stored in MongoDB.

Conceptually, a message contains information such as:

```text
Message
├── trip
├── sender
├── content
├── type
├── mediaUrl
├── createdAt
└── other metadata
```

The database provides persistence.

Socket.IO provides real-time delivery.

Together:

```text
MongoDB
   +
Socket.IO
   =
Persistent Real-Time Chat
```

---

# 🔄 Loading Previous Messages

When a user opens a trip chat, previous messages need to be displayed.

The flow is:

```text
Open Trip
   ↓
Chat Component Mounts
   ↓
Request Previous Messages
   ↓
Backend
   ↓
MongoDB
   ↓
Messages Returned
   ↓
React State
   ↓
Messages Rendered
```

After the historical messages are loaded, Socket.IO handles new messages.

Therefore the chat has two communication mechanisms:

```text
REST API
   → Historical/Persistent Data

Socket.IO
   → Real-Time Updates
```

This combination is an important architectural pattern.

